 "method": "Mean travel time = aggregate travel time to work in minutes (B08013_001) / workers 16 and over who did not work from home (B08134_001). This reproduces the Census Bureau's published 'mean travel time to work' (table S0801). Margin of error from the published margins with the Census Bureau's formula for ratios. Citywide figures are for New York city (place 3651000); peers are matched on place GEOID (state + place FIPS) from data/peers.json; borough figures are the five counties.",
 "caveats": [
  "People who work from home are excluded. Remote work rose sharply after 2019, so the mix of people who commute changed; 2021 onward is not strictly comparable to earlier years.",
  "Travel time is self-reported for the week before the survey, door to door, one way.",
  "The ACS 1-year 2020 standard tables were never released (the Census Bureau published only experimental estimates because of pandemic data-collection problems), so 2020 is missing from every ACS series.",
  "The 2005 ACS covered only people in households; group quarters (dorms, prisons, nursing homes, shelters) were added in 2006. 2005 values are not strictly comparable to later years.",
  "All ACS figures are survey estimates. moe is the 90 percent margin of error, published by the Census Bureau for single cells and derived with the Bureau's approximation formulas for sums, shares and ratios.",
  "Margins of error: for 2010, 2011, 2012, 2013, 2014, 2015, 2016, 2017, 2018, 2019, 2021, 2022, 2023, 2024, 2025 the moe is the one the Census Bureau publishes in subject table S0801 (which matches this estimate to its published rounding) for the city, the 2025 peers and the boroughs. Other years, and the race breakdowns, use the derived margin described in the method."
 ]
}
